const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Translation with Editing","serviceType":"Translation service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/translation-with-editing/","description":"Research translation with independent revision by a second linguist, built to the ISO 17100 structure. $0.12 a word, from $240. 500 words translated free.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I get a research paper translated into English for journal submission?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Send the complete source document, name the target journal, and say whether a machine has already been through it. The work then runs in six stages: terminology agreed before drafting, translation by a qualified translator in your subject, revision against the source by a second linguist, a monolingual review by an editor in your discipline, formatting to the journal, and a final verification.\n\nA 6,000-word paper is typically $720 in six to ten business days, and you receive the translation, the glossary and a certificate on request. Decide the target journal early — it sets word limits, reference style and abstract headings, and retrofitting those to a finished translation is slower than translating into them."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does this cost more than $0.05 a word?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because a second qualified linguist reads every sentence against the source, and that person has to be paid.\n\nA cheaper quote almost always buys a single translator checking their own work. That is a different product rather than a cheaper version of this one: a translator cannot reliably catch the places where they misread the source, which is exactly what the reviser is there for.\n\nAnything at $0.01 to $0.03 a word is raw machine output or light post-editing of it. That is a legitimate product with a name and a standard of its own; we sell it, priced as post-editing, and we do not call it translation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you guarantee 100% accuracy, or that the journal will accept the paper?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No to both.\n\nOn accuracy: ISO 5060:2024, the international standard for evaluating translation output, works through an error typology with error-type weights, severity levels and rules for counting repeated errors. A standard written to help you weight and count errors assumes errors occur.\n\nOn acceptance: that is an editor’s decision about novelty, design, fit and significance, and no supplier controls it.\n\nWhat we do guarantee is bounded and inside our control: if you are not satisfied with the quality of the translation, we re-translate it at no added cost."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a certified translation, and do I need one?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A certified translation is a property of the document, not a qualification held by the translator, and the answer changes by country.\n\nIn the United States, 8 CFR 103.2(b)(3) asks only for the translator’s own certification of completeness and accuracy. The United Kingdom has no sworn-translator system; a dated, signed certificate naming the translator is the norm. Civil-law jurisdictions such as Spain, Germany and France appoint sworn translators through the courts — we say plainly when your recipient needs one and we cannot provide it.\n\nTwo things: ISO 17100 certifies organisations, not individuals, so no translator is “ISO 17100 certified”; and no major publisher we found requires a certificate for a journal submission. We provide one free on request."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"We already ran it through DeepL or ChatGPT. Is that cheaper to finish?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Sometimes, and sometimes the opposite — which is why we ask.\n\nMachine output reads fluently and fails invisibly: confident, grammatical English that inverts a negation, drops a hedge, or renders a unit wrongly. A translator handed that without being told corrects at the sentence level — exactly the level at which those errors do not show.\n\nTold what it is, they work under ISO 18587, a different standard from ISO 17100, which excludes machine translation plus post-editing. Full post-editing is $0.06 a word against $0.12, so when the output is decent it is half the price. When it is poor, translating afresh is faster and cheaper; we tell you which case you are in after reading a sample."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you do back-translation to check the quality?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not as a general quality check, because it is not one. Back-translation appears in no translation standard — not ISO 17100, not 18587, not 5060.\n\nIt belongs in linguistic validation of patient-reported outcome measures, as one stage in the ISPOR task force method: forward translation by two independent translators, reconciliation, back-translation, review, cognitive debriefing with target-language patients, and proofreading. There it does real work: the instrument must measure the same construct in every language.\n\nSold as reassurance on an ordinary manuscript it misleads: a back-translation that differs from the source often signals a good idiomatic translation, not a bad one. If you need linguistic validation it is a separate service at $2,900 per language per instrument."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Editing and translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Editing and Translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/translation-with-editing/"}]}];

